     llggaammmmaa(_x), llggaammmmaaff(_x), and llggaammmmaall(_x) return ln|Γ(x)|.  The external in‐
     teger _s_i_g_n_g_a_m returns the sign of Γ(x).

     llggaammmmaa__rr(_x, _s_i_g_n_g_a_m_p), llggaammmmaaff__rr(_x, _s_i_g_n_g_a_m_p), and llggaammmmaall__rr(_x, _s_i_g_n_g_a_m_p)
     provide the same functionality as llggaammmmaa(_x), llggaammmmaaff(_x), and llggaammmmaall(_x),
     but the caller must provide an integer to store the sign of Γ(x).

     The ttggaammmmaa(_x), ttggaammmmaaff(_x), and ttggaammmmaall(_x) functions return Γ(x), with no
     effect on _s_i_g_n_g_a_m.

     ggaammmmaa(), ggaammmmaaff(), ggaammmmaa__rr(), and ggaammmmaaff__rr() are deprecated aliases for
     llggaammmmaa(), llggaammmmaaff(), llggaammmmaa__rr(), and llggaammmmaaff__rr(), respectively.

IIDDIIOOSSYYNNCCRRAASSIIEESS
     Do not use the expression “signgam∗exp(lgamma(x))” to compute g := Γ(x).
     Instead use a program like this (in C):

           lg = lgamma(x); g = signgam∗exp(lg);

     Only after llggaammmmaa() or llggaammmmaaff() has returned can signgam be correct.

     For arguments in its range, ttggaammmmaa() is preferred, as for positive argu‐
     ments it is accurate to within one unit in the last place.  Exponentia‐
     tion of llggaammmmaa() will lose up to 10 significant bits.

